hey everybody and welcome to planet zoo  this will be my first blended zoo  time-lapse  so it's kind of a special moment but at  the same time this is a very simple  video and quite sure if you compare it  to my usual videos you're probably  wondering why this is less than ten  minutes I mean how am I gonna make any  money off of mid-roll ads if I'm not  even reaching ten minutes and besides  there's not too much that you can do in  ten minutes but this is really the kind  of content that I want to try and make  for the planet zubeida given the fact  that the beta will only last for two  weeks and after that there will be  somewhat of a radio silence where we  can't play the game anymore so whatever  I do I want to keep my projects quite  short and simple for the beta just to  explore the pieces see what's possible  in the game and of course also trying to  make some small zoo elements maybe I'll  make a small two or three parter series  I'm just gonna have to see what I feel  like doing but whatever I'm going to do  I want to keep everything quite simple  and short just to make sure that I don't  run out of time until the end of the  beta and besides we might lose  everything that we do now when the full  game comes out frontier isn't sure  whether they're gonna be able to support  blueprints coming out in the beta in the  full game so I'm gonna have to build  everything with the assumption that I  won't be able to save it and I'm gonna  lose it at some point so for that reason  I just want to feel a bit more free to  just kind of do whatever I feel like  doing in the spur of the moment and in  this case that was a Japanese teahouse  so I'm building a Japanese teahouse and  that's basically it won't be ending  anything around it really or I won't be  building it on it in any way now I  should probably talk a little bit about  this tea house what it is and why I  decided to build a Japanese teahouse  well for one I guess at this point a  little bit infamous for building  something Japanese in every simulation  game I come across and I tried to  restrain myself when it comes to planets  ooh but in the end as you can see it  only took one day for me to build  something Japanese so we're already here  we got this over with and now that the  Japanese theme is all done I guess I can  move on to other ideas so partly it's  just because I really wanted to do it  partly it's also because I realized that  it's doable because well when the game  came out and when I was doing my live  stream for a few hours just recording  some of my first gameplay I didn't  realize that in the franchise mode which  I'm currently in you can actually  research scenery pieces and pretty much  get all of the scenery pieces that are  available in the beta so even though we  don't have a sandbox mode in the beta  this is as close to a sandbox mode as  you can get I think given the fact that  we can get all of the pieces and it's a  completely flat map with some money even  though it's very limited so I have to  watch out regarding my budget it's  somewhat of a sandbox mode so I think  that works alright and when I was  looking at the pieces that I researched  specifically the Indian ones actually  because that's where I got the tile  roofs from that you can see at the top  here I realized that Japanese  architecture would probably be doable  and so even though it's not in the game  at least not at the moment I have no  idea about full release although it  would be really cool to see an East  Asian wall pack of some sort  I figured for now it's probably possible  and especially the type of building  which I'm building here which is if I  pronounced it correctly a cheat suit  which is a traditional tea house and  these buildings tend to be quite subdued  very simple well not necessarily simple  but not overly detailed or well with  ridiculous curved Asian roofs as they  are stereotypically known for or any  detailing or even painted elements  everything is quite simple  using just natural elements and textures  a lot comes down to how you place the  details rather than just how much you  place down and the general composition  of the building at the end of the day  they are typically very simple buildings  just for tea ceremonies with a lot of  Shoji walls that you can move around  Shoji being this combination of Japanese  paper and bamboo making these grid like  patterns of windows walls and doors that  you can move around on the floor to  rearrange the layouts of buildings  because in a lot of traditional Japanese  architecture you don't really have the  solid walls that you see in Western  architecture buildings are much more  flexible much more open and they can  move a lot of the walls around and  especially on a good day open the entire  building sets something that should  realistically also be possible for the  building which I'm building right now  that's why I am making it look the way  that I am right now and making some of  these Shoji screens using his stucco  pieces and wooden beams which is not the  best solution to try and make these but  I think especially from a distance it  looks convincing enough now I will be  closing this entire building just  because I don't want to deal with  interiors or anything that would be  really difficult given the fact that the  interior is kind of a warbot mess of  different wooden posts or bumbling into  each other just because I needed them  like that to make the exterior so I'm  just closing the whole building off but  I'm trying to make it look as though we  have all of these walls and doors and  windows that can actually move around  and rearrange the building  and typically in real life I think you  find a lot of these buildings in  Japanese gardens as well be it in a tea  garden which is typically just a single  path winding through a gun and toward a  tea house or one of these lake style  gardens where you have a path walking  around the lake and you'll often find 